dockplane-agent for Debian and Ubuntu
=====================================

The package installs the agent and its service unit. It does not enroll the
host and does not start the service, because an agent with no identity has
nothing to connect to.

Enrolling
---------

  1. Create an enrollment token in Dockplane (Agents → Create enrollment
     token). It is shown once, is single-use and expires in ten minutes.

  2. Hand it to the agent, as the account the service runs as:

       sudo -u dockplane-agent \
         DOCKPLANE_AGENT_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/dockplane-agent \
         dockplane-agent enroll --server https://dockplane.example.com --token-stdin

     There is deliberately no --token flag: a token on a command line is
     visible in the process list to every user on the machine and is written
     to the shell history. Use --token-stdin, --token-file, the
     DOCKPLANE_ENROLLMENT_TOKEN environment variable, or the prompt.

  3. Start it:

       sudo systemctl enable --now dockplane-agent

     The host appears in Dockplane within a few seconds.

Docker socket access
--------------------

The unit gives the service account membership of the `docker` group, which is
how the agent reaches the Engine API.

**Membership of the `docker` group is equivalent to root on this host.**
Anything able to talk to the Docker daemon can start a privileged container
that mounts the filesystem. This is unavoidable for managing Docker, and it is
the reason the agent has no way to run a command: its set of operations is
fixed at build time, and the three that change anything — start, stop and
restart a container — take a container identifier and nothing else.

Do not expose the Docker daemon over unauthenticated TCP as an alternative.

Files
-----

  /usr/bin/dockplane-agent                        the binary
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/dockplane-agent.service the unit
  /etc/dockplane-agent/agent.env                  optional settings (conffile)
  /var/lib/dockplane-agent/                       identity and private key, 0700

The state directory is created by systemd on first start and is owned by the
service account. Nothing in it is shipped by this package.

Upgrading
---------

  sudo dpkg -i dockplane-agent_<version>_<arch>.deb

The identity, the private key and /etc/dockplane-agent survive. The agent
keeps the same agent ID and does not need enrolling again; a running service
is restarted onto the new binary. Downgrades are only supported between
versions that speak the same protocol version — see `dockplane-agent version`.

Removing
--------

  sudo apt remove dockplane-agent    keeps /var/lib/dockplane-agent
  sudo apt purge  dockplane-agent    deletes it

After a purge the host has no identity and must be enrolled again. Revoke the
old agent in Dockplane as well: its certificate stays valid until you do.
